@endnode

@node layout "Layout"

installer/
+-- ZZ9000Installer.info              Drawer icon
+-- ZZ9000Installer/
    +-- Install ZZ9000                Installer script (what the user double-clicks)
    +-- Install ZZ9000.info           Script icon
    +-- Tools.info                    Sub-drawer icon
    +-- Docs/
    |   +-- ahi.guide                Audio tunables doc generated by CI
    |   +-- ZZTop.guide              ZZTop control-panel manual generated by CI
    |   +-- tools.guide              Command-line tool manual generated by CI
    |   +-- usb-poseidon.guide       Poseidon setup doc generated by CI
    |   +-- ZZCapture.guide          Native capture diagnostic manual generated by CI
    |   +-- sdk.guide                SDK runtime doc generated by CI
    |   +-- ZZPlay.guide             ZZPlay manual from the SDK payload
    |   +-- amissl.guide             Accelerated AmiSSL doc generated by CI
    +-- Devs/
    |   +-- Picasso96Settings         Zorro II-safe P96 screenmode config  (committed)
    |   +-- Picasso96Settings-Z3      Zorro III high-memory P96 config  (committed)
    |   +-- USBHardware/              <- zzusbhw.device populated by CI
    |   +-- NetInterfaces/
    |   |   +-- ZZ9000Net             Roadshow NetInterface template  (committed)
    |   +-- AHI/                      <- zz9000ax.audio populated by CI
    |   +-- AudioModes/               <- ZZ9000AX populated by CI
    |   +-- Networks/                 <- ZZ9000Net.device populated by CI
    +-- Classes/
    |   +-- DataTypes/               <- zz9k-picture.datatype populated by CI
    +-- Storage/
    |   +-- DataTypes/               <- JPEG/PNG datatype descriptors populated by CI
    +-- Libs/
    |   +-- MHI/                      <- mhizz9000.library populated by CI
    |   +-- Picasso96/                <- ZZ9000.card populated by CI
    |   +-- zz9k.library             <- populated by CI
    |   +-- mpega.library            <- populated by CI
    |   +-- AmiSSL/
    |       +-- 68020-40/            <- amissl_v362.library (68020/030/040) populated by CI
    |       +-- 68060/               <- amissl_v362.library (68060) populated by CI
    +-- Tools/
        +-- ZZTop.info                Icon  (committed, generated by
        |                                    tools/make-zztop-icon.py)
        +--                           <- ZZTop, ZZScanlines, ZZFwUpdate,
                                        ZZNetStats, ZZDiag, ZZCapture,
                                        zz9k-info, zz9k-services, zz9k-view,
                                        zz9k-mp3, zz9k-cryptobench,
                                        zz9k-archive,
                                        ZZPlay + ZZPlay.info
                                        populated by CI

Only @{b}icons and configuration templates@{ub} are committed. Every binary payload
is built by CI and copied into this tree during the release assembly step
(see @{b}.github/workflows/ci.yml@{ub}, @{b}release@{ub} job). The @{b}.gitignore@{ub} rules on
@{b}*.card@{ub}, @{b}*.device@{ub}, @{b}*.audio@{ub}, etc. keep any locally-populated binaries from
accidentally being committed back.

This means a fresh @{b}git clone@{ub} will @{b}not@{ub} produce a runnable Installer drawer
on its own - CI must have populated it, or you must copy the binaries in by
hand.

@{b}ZZ9000.card@{ub} 2.4 and newer uses the native @{b}BT_MNT_ZZ9000@{ub} Picasso96 board
type. Upgrade tests should copy one of the committed
@{b}ZZ9000Installer/Devs/Picasso96Settings*@{ub} files along with the new RTG
driver; old settings saved under the legacy @{b}uaegfx@{ub} board type are not
guaranteed to attach to the new driver identity. The default
@{b}Picasso96Settings@{ub} profile is safe for Zorro II and Zorro III. The
@{b}Picasso96Settings-Z3@{ub} profile enables 1920x1080x32 and should only be
installed on Zorro III systems. Both profiles advertise the monitor's
Picasso96 DPMS Standby, Suspend, and Active Off capabilities so DPMS
utilities can reach the firmware-gated @{b}ZZ9000.card@{ub} callback. The installer
backs up the previous settings file as
@{b}Devs:Picasso96Settings.pre-ZZ9000-2.4@{ub} before installing the migrated
settings file. The current 1920x1080 timing uses a 148571429 Hz pixel clock
in both stock templates, matching the updated firmware and RTG callbacks.
Preserve a backup of custom settings; do not overwrite them merely to test
the native Scandoubler output.

Native Amiga chipset video normally defaults to @{b}filtered_60@{ub}: PAL input uses
800x600 and NTSC input uses 720x480, both at nominal 60 Hz. The
nonstandard-sync firmware variant defaults to PAL timing. In ZZTop, open
@{b}Settings -> Scandoubler...@{ub}, select @{b}Output@{ub} and @{b}Refresh@{ub}, then @{b}Save@{ub} and
power-cycle. @{b}Capture...@{ub} in that window owns sampling, framing and
calibration; the @{b}Other Settings...@{ub} window no longer contains native-video
controls. The Settings menu also opens Audio and Other Settings when the
main buttons are off-screen.

Centered 1920x1080 output preserves the 1280x1024 native picture and offers
nominal 60 Hz or 50 Hz only with matching firmware/bitstream capabilities.
Install the matching @{b}ZZ9000.card@{ub} and ZZTop as well. These choices are
separate from Picasso96 RTG screenmodes; see the profile table and
compatibility rules (../README.md#scandoubler-output-and-refresh). Remove
legacy @{b}ENVARC:ZZ9000-VCAP-800x600@{ub}, @{b}ENVARC:ZZ9000-NS-VSYNC@{ub}, and
@{b}ENVARC:ZZ9000-NS-VSYNC-NTSC@{ub} overrides when using ZZTop to manage native
output.

Release packaging converts the README, manuals, and installer script to
Amiga-compatible ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1), replacing Unicode punctuation and
diagrams with plain-text equivalents. Repository sources remain UTF-8.

The SDK runtime payloads (@{b}zz9k.library@{ub}, the ARM-accelerated @{b}mpega.library@{ub}
drop-in, the @{b}zz9k-picture.datatype@{ub} plus its inactive JPEG/PNG descriptors
under @{b}Storage/DataTypes@{ub}, and the @{b}zz9k-#?@{ub} CLI tools) are built from the
pinned zz9000-sdk ref and populated by CI. They need the SDK-service ZZ9000
firmware to do anything useful. On Zorro 2 boards, current matched
firmware, bitstream, SDK payloads, and drivers negotiate a shared 64 KiB
host window. It supports compact audio/MP3, streamed image/DataType,
archive, and AmiSSL clients on the shipped 2 MiB and 4 MiB profiles. The 4
MiB profile also provides one bounded 224 KiB ZZPlay PIP source; the 2 MiB
profile has no video PIP. Keep the complete release set matched: invalid or
unacknowledged generation-1 descriptors disable the negotiated paths.
Descriptor-absent legacy 4 MiB compatibility retains only the historical
fixed 64 KiB host window, without negotiated layout or PIP; legacy 2 MiB
and unknown sizes reject it. The host window is shared across clients, so
concurrent accelerated operations can contend.

@{b}ZZCapture@{ub} is offered as a separate, optional CLI-tool choice. Its @{b}info@{ub} and
@{b}observe@{ub} commands collect native-video timing evidence without changing the
capture phase. The @{b}phase@{ub}, @{b}check@{ub}, @{b}startup@{ub}, and @{b}calibrate@{ub} commands are
experimental A4000/C28 diagnostics and require the exact matched firmware
and bitstream plus @{b}Stack 32768@{ub}. Read @{b}Docs/ZZCapture.guide@{ub} before using
them; a matched-firmware refusal is a package/version problem, not a check
to bypass.

When installing the ZZ9000-accelerated @{b}amissl.library@{ub}, the installer first
backs up the stock @{b}LIBS:AmiSSL/amissl_v362.library@{ub} to
@{b}amissl_v362.library.bak@{ub} - only if no @{b}.bak@{ub} already exists, so re-running the
installer always preserves the genuine original - then installs the build
matching the host CPU (@{b}Libs/AmiSSL/68020-40/@{ub} for 68020/030/040 and the
Apollo 68080, @{b}Libs/AmiSSL/68060/@{ub} for the 68060), detected exactly as
AmiSSL's own installer does. To revert, delete the accelerated
@{b}amissl_v362.library@{ub} and rename the @{b}.bak@{ub} back. The accelerated library
covers supported TLS handshake and record crypto; unsupported algorithms
automatically stay on AmiSSL's software provider.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node trying-the-installer-locally "Trying the installer locally"
If you want to test the installer with your own local builds (rather than
downloading a release zip), copy each artifact into the drawer using the
same layout CI uses:

SDK_SRC=../zz9000-sdk          # a zz9000-sdk checkout; supplies scripts/md2guide.py
INST=installer/ZZ9000Installer
install -Dm644 rtg/ZZ9000.card                 "$INST/Libs/Picasso96/ZZ9000.card"
install -Dm644 mhi/mhizz9000.library           "$INST/Libs/MHI/mhizz9000.library"
install -Dm644 usb-poseidon/zzusbhw.device     "$INST/Devs/USBHardware/zzusbhw.device"
install -Dm644 net/ZZ9000Net.device            "$INST/Devs/Networks/ZZ9000Net.device"
install -Dm644 ahi/driver/zz9000ax.audio       "$INST/Devs/AHI/zz9000ax.audio"
install -Dm644 ahi/driver/ZZ9000AX             "$INST/Devs/AudioModes/ZZ9000AX"
install -Dm755 ZZTop/ZZTop                     "$INST/Tools/ZZTop"
install -Dm755 ZZScanlines/ZZScanlines         "$INST/Tools/ZZScanlines"
install -Dm755 ZZFwUpdate/ZZFwUpdate           "$INST/Tools/ZZFwUpdate"
install -Dm755 net/ZZNetStats/ZZNetStats       "$INST/Tools/ZZNetStats"
install -Dm755 ZZDiag/ZZDiag                   "$INST/Tools/ZZDiag"
install -Dm755 ZZCapture/ZZCapture             "$INST/Tools/ZZCapture"
python3 "$SDK_SRC/scripts/md2guide.py" --name ahi ahi/README.md          "$INST/Docs/ahi.guide"
python3 "$SDK_SRC/scripts/md2guide.py" --name usb-poseidon \
  usb-poseidon/README.md                                                "$INST/Docs/usb-poseidon.guide"
python3 "$SDK_SRC/scripts/md2guide.py" --name ZZCapture \
  ZZCapture/README.md                                                   "$INST/Docs/ZZCapture.guide"

The SDK runtime payloads and the per-CPU accelerated @{b}amissl_v362.library@{ub}
are not produced by the lines above: they come from @{b}sdk/out@{ub} and @{b}amissl/out@{ub}
after their own builds. The easiest way to stage everything (drivers, SDK
payloads, and both amissl builds) into the drawer exactly as CI does is
@{b}make package-local@{ub}, which runs @{b}tools/package-local.sh@{ub}.

Then copy the @{b}installer/@{ub} tree onto your Amiga and double-click @{b}Install
ZZ9000@{ub}.

For a direct RTG-only test, copy @{b}rtg/ZZ9000.card@{ub} to @{b}Libs:Picasso96/@{ub} and
copy @{b}installer/ZZ9000Installer/Devs/Picasso96Settings@{ub} to @{b}Devs:@{ub}.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node release-bundle-layout "Release bundle layout"
The CI release job (tag push @{b}v*@{ub}) produces a zip with the installer contents
at the package root:

zz9000-drivers-<tag>/
+-- README.md
+-- ZZ9000Installer.info
+-- ZZ9000Installer/

Driver and tool binaries are not duplicated as loose files at the zip root;
they live in the exact drawer paths consumed by @{b}Install ZZ9000@{ub}.

The release job also copies component docs into @{b}ZZ9000Installer/Docs/@{ub}:
@{b}ahi-README.md@{ub} covers the remaining audio ENV tunable (@{b}ZZ9K_INT2@{ub}) and the
removed @{b}ZZ9K_MIX_LEVELS@{ub} / @{b}ZZ9000AX-NOLPF@{ub} variables, @{b}usb-poseidon-README.md@{ub}
covers Poseidon registration and troubleshooting, @{b}sdk-README.md@{ub} documents
the SDK runtime payloads, @{b}ZZCapture-README.md@{ub} documents the native capture
diagnostic and its matched firmware requirement, and @{b}amissl-README.md@{ub}
documents the accelerated AmiSSL build.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node what-doesn-t-go-through-this-installer "What doesn't go through this Installer"
The CI-built artifact below is @{b}not@{ub} handled by this Installer script because
it doesn't live on the AmigaOS filesystem:

@{b}Artifact    | Install path                                                 @{ub}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@{b}zzsd.device@{ub} | Packed into firmware @{b}BOOT.bin@{ub} - see sd-boot/README.md        
            | (../sd-boot/README.md).                                      

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode

@node updating-the-script "Updating the script"
- The script lives at @{b}installer/ZZ9000Installer/Install ZZ9000@{ub} and is plain
  text (Commodore Installer LISP-like syntax).

- If you add a new artifact, add both:

  - A @{b}(copyfiles ...)@{ub} step in the script with the right @{b}(source ...)@{ub} path
    inside the drawer and @{b}(dest ...)@{ub} on the target filesystem.

  - A matching @{b}install -Dm...@{ub} line in the @{b}release@{ub} job of
    @{b}.github/workflows/ci.yml@{ub} so the binary actually lands in the drawer
    before zipping.

@{"Contents" link Main}

@endnode
